  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface ridging continues to nose into the area today. Valley\nfog will begin to lift with daytime heating, but otherwise it is\nexpected to be dry this afternoon. While the center of surface\nhigh pressure is forecast to be south of the region, it will\nexert its influence locally. Temps should radiate nicely\novernight, which given the pattern of late the NBM guidance is\npicking up on. The upper ridge will also slip east of the area \nsometime this evening, and allow return flow to begin. This may\nmake marine fog/stratus a little more widespread overnight, but\nsouthwest winds may also keep that banked more towards the\nDowneast coastline than the Midcoast.\n\nA stray shower can't be ruled out Thursday morning as a warm \nfront lifts through New England, with moisture increasing \nthrough the daytime. The best chance is over New Hampshire as \nMaine looks to remain on the periphery of high pressure \ndeparting to the east. High temperatures climb into the low 80s \njust about everywhere, with dewpoints in the mid-60s making it \nfeel somewhat sticky.\n\nThe first of two cold fronts attempts to make a pass Thursday \nnight. which will increase PoPs for the western half of the CWA,\nwith lesser chances further east. Scattered showers and storms \ndecrease in coverage through the evening, but will remain \npossible through the overnight hours still. Areas of fog and \ndrizzle will be likely as the first front fails to bring a \nchange of airmasses. It will take a second front during the day \non Friday to usher in a cooler and drier airmass late in the day\nand Friday night. With the front's approach and passage, \nscattered showers and storms will be possible again during the \nday on Friday, with the best chance across the higher terrain \nand western areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... The timing of the front's \nprogression came in a little faster on this evening's models \nruns, hedging towards cooler and drier air arriving early enough\nto drop lows into the 40s and 50s by Saturday morning. Drier \nair continues moving in on Saturday with high pressure building \nin at the surface. This makes for a mostly sunny and mostly dry \nweekend with high temperatures in the mid-70s to low 80s and \ndewpoints in the 50s across much of the area.\n\nGlobal models suggest at least one upper low moves through \nQuebec Sunday making its closest pass Sunday night which may \ndrag a front through the region early next week. There is still \nplenty of uncertainty among the global models on exactly how \nthis plays out, but I think the chance PoPs from the NBM are \nfair. A warming trend then looks likely early next week as the \nhigh pressure center moves off the East Coast and ridging \nbuilds into the Northeast.",
